#{[17592186540023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540023 #edb/ref 1071 "$6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0be7ea7e-f479-5c43-ae90-b193d0f73efd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540023 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =6({log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{2}⁡y) \\\\  & =6{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})+6{log}_{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${log}_{5}⁡s=4$ and ${log}_{5}⁡t=5,$ then ${log}_{5}⁡(st)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35a5df48-5462-5c58-bbc0-60fbb99c3f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540043 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(st) & ={log}_{5}⁡s+{log}_{5}⁡t \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36bfe507-bf36-5e20-b82e-5099f50d12ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540044 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =8(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1071 "Express ${log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a84d4a4-1873-5e3e-810d-d5ca7ce87d53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540045 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(8\\cdot 3)+{log}_{6}⁡2 \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(24\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=10$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=2$, then $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ea44857-e35f-51f3-a0fe-716b02fb3232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540050 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =10+2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56e99923-e71e-5783-b293-6f220455cbaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540052 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5 & =5({log}_{3}⁡4+{log}_{3}⁡5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡(4\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =5{log}_{3}⁡20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5{log}_{3}⁡4+5{log}_{3}⁡5=5{log}_{3}⁡20.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1071 "${log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64aa673c-cb55-5490-a5e1-8f5f8d78ed0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540054 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2})={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $32={2}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡32={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(32{x}^{2}) & ={log}_{2}⁡32+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5+{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8$ as a single logarithm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ea8b2b1-a8ee-55af-9a04-8ea83c2d0aaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540076 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8 & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+\\operatorname{log}⁡8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\cdot 4\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{log}⁡2+4\\operatorname{log}⁡4+4\\operatorname{log}⁡8=4\\operatorname{log}⁡64.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1071 "${log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c529b9a8-1cea-5cb5-8b9e-8f76c8202de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540092 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3})={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16={4}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡16={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(16{x}^{3}) & ={log}_{4}⁡16+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2+{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the following expression as a single logarithm.\n\n$5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7c12aa8-de03-59c9-ab5d-5e64e226cea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540094 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p\\cdot q\\cdot pq) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({p}^{2}{q}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the following expression as a single logarithm.\n\n$4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})=4\\operatorname{log}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f62edb2c-be7a-51bc-a4d3-e51bf196542b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540109 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡t+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3}) & =4(\\operatorname{log}⁡t+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{1+2+3}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66559" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fba4c35a-120c-5bd8-b872-2cfc6815a465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540112 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100={10}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡100=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡100+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1071 "$9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbe8f3d1-3c93-5a68-ac60-38c193b6c6b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540113 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms and the distributive law, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{log}⁡(6z) & =9(\\operatorname{log}⁡6+\\operatorname{log}⁡z) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{log}⁡6+9\\operatorname{log}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
